VIII. Duties of the People's Procuratorate During the Review of Prosecution Stage
26. Notification of Rights. After a case is transferred for review of prosecution, the People's Procuratorate shall inform the criminal suspect of the procedural rights he or she enjoys and the legal provisions on admitting guilt and accepting punishment, and shall safeguard the criminal suspect's right to choose the procedure. The notification shall be in writing, and shall be fully explained when necessary.
27. Seeking Opinions. If a criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment, the People's Procuratorate shall seek the opinions of the criminal suspect, defender, or duty lawyer on the following matters, and shall record them in the case file and attach them:
(1) The suspected criminal facts, charges, and applicable legal provisions;
(2) Recommendations for lenient punishment, such as a lighter or mitigated punishment, or exemption from punishment;
(3) The procedure applicable to the trial of the case after the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment;
(4) Other circumstances requiring opinions.
If the People's Procuratorate does not adopt the opinions of the defender or duty lawyer, it shall state the reasons.
28. Review of Voluntariness and Legality. For cases where the criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment during the investigation stage, the People's Procuratorate shall focus on reviewing the following:
(1) Whether the criminal suspect voluntarily admits guilt and accepts punishment, and whether there is any instance of being compelled to admit guilt and accept punishment against his or her will due to violence, threats, or inducements;
(2) Whether the cognitive ability and mental state of the criminal suspect at the time of admitting guilt and accepting punishment were normal;
(3) Whether the criminal suspect understands the nature of admitting guilt and accepting punishment and the possible legal consequences;
(4) Whether the investigative authority informed the criminal suspect of the procedural rights he or she enjoys, the legal provisions that truthful confession may lead to lenient treatment and the legal provisions on admitting guilt and accepting punishment, and sought opinions;
(5) Whether the opinion on prosecution states the circumstance that the criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment;
(6) Whether the criminal suspect genuinely repents and whether he or she has apologized to the victim.
If, upon review, it is found that the criminal suspect admitted guilt and accepted punishment against his or her will, the People's Procuratorate may re-initiate the work related to admitting guilt and accepting punishment. If there are acts of illegal evidence collection such as extortion of confessions by torture, they shall be handled in accordance with the law.
29. Discovery of Evidence. The People's Procuratorate may, based on the specific circumstances of the case, explore the system of evidence discovery to safeguard the criminal suspect's right to information and the authenticity and voluntariness of the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment.
30. Application of Non-Prosecution. The prosecutorial discretion shall be improved, the pre-trial diversion and filtering function of non-prosecution shall be given full play, and the application of conditional non-prosecution in cases involving admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment shall be gradually expanded. For a minor criminal case that is undisputed after the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment and does not require a criminal penalty, the People's Procuratorate may make a decision of non-prosecution according to law. The People's Procuratorate shall strengthen the prediction of sentencing in cases, and for minor criminal cases where exemption from punishment may be imposed, it may make a decision of non-prosecution according to law.
For cases where the facts are unclear or the evidence is insufficient after the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, a decision of non-prosecution shall be made according to law.
31. Signing the Affidavit of Admission of Guilt and Acceptance of Punishment. If a criminal suspect voluntarily admits guilt and agrees to the sentencing recommendation and the applicable procedure, he or she shall sign the affidavit of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment in the presence of a defender or duty lawyer. If the criminal suspect is in custody, the detention center shall provide a place for signing the affidavit. The affidavit shall include the criminal suspect's truthful confession of the offense, agreement to the sentencing recommendation, and application of the procedure, and shall be signed by the criminal suspect, defender, or duty lawyer.
If a criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment, an affidavit of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment is not required to be signed in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The criminal suspect is blind, deaf, mute, or a person with a mental illness who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his or her behavior;
(2) The legal representative or defender of a minor criminal suspect objects to the minor's admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment;
(3) Other circumstances where signing the affidavit is not required.
If the criminal suspect does not sign the affidavit of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment under the circumstances above, this shall not affect the application of the leniency system for admitting guilt and accepting punishment.
32. Initiation of Public Prosecution. When the People's Procuratorate initiates a public prosecution in the People's Court, it shall state in the indictment the circumstance that the defendant admits guilt and accepts punishment, propose a sentencing recommendation, and transfer materials including the affidavit of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment. The sentencing recommendation letter may be made separately or stated in the indictment.
33. Proposal of Sentencing Recommendations. If a criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment, the People's Procuratorate shall propose a sentencing recommendation regarding the principal punishment, supplementary punishment, and whether probation shall apply. Before proposing a sentencing recommendation, the People's Procuratorate shall fully hear the opinions of the criminal suspect, defender, or duty lawyer, and shall try to reach a consensus.
In handling a case involving admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, the People's Procuratorate shall generally propose a fixed-term sentencing recommendation. For new types of crimes or uncommon crimes, or serious crimes with complex sentencing circumstances, a sentencing recommendation with a range may also be proposed. When proposing a sentencing recommendation, the reasons and basis shall be stated.
If the criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment without any other statutory sentencing circumstances, the People's Procuratorate may, based on the facts and nature of the crime, propose a fixed-term sentencing recommendation with an appropriate reduction from the benchmark sentence. If there are other statutory sentencing circumstances, the People's Procuratorate shall comprehensively consider the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment together with other statutory sentencing circumstances, and propose a fixed-term sentencing recommendation by reference to relevant sentencing guidelines.
If the criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment during the investigation stage, the range of leniency for the principal punishment may be appropriately expanded on the basis of the preceding paragraph; if the defendant admits guilt and accepts punishment during the trial stage, the range may be appropriately reduced on the basis of the preceding paragraph. If a recommendation for a fine is made, a fixed amount shall be proposed by reference to the range of leniency for the principal punishment.
34. Time Limit for Handling Cases Using the Fast-Track Procedure. If a criminal suspect admits guilt and accepts punishment, and the People's Procuratorate, upon review, finds that the conditions for applying the fast-track procedure are met, it shall make a decision on whether to initiate public prosecution within ten days; if the likely sentence of fixed-term imprisonment exceeds one year, the decision may be made within fifteen days.

X. Trial Procedures and Duties of the People's Court
39. Review of the Voluntariness and Legality of the Admission of Guilt and Acceptance of Punishment During the Trial Stage. When handling a case involving admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, the People's Court shall inform the defendant of the procedural rights he or she enjoys and the legal provisions on admitting guilt and accepting punishment, and shall hear the opinions of the defendant and his or her defender or duty lawyer. During the trial, the voluntariness of the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment and the authenticity and legality of the contents of the affidavit shall be examined and verified, with a focus on verifying the following:
(1) Whether the defendant voluntarily admits guilt and accepts punishment, and whether there is any instance of being compelled to admit guilt and accept punishment against his or her will due to violence, threats, or inducements;
(2) Whether the cognitive ability and mental state of the defendant at the time of admitting guilt and accepting punishment were normal;
(3) Whether the defendant understands the nature of admitting guilt and accepting punishment and the possible legal consequences;
(4) Whether the People's Procuratorate and public security organ fulfilled the obligation of notification and sought opinions;
(5) Whether the duty lawyer or defender communicated with the People's Procuratorate, provided effective legal assistance or defense, and witnessed the signing of the affidavit of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment.
During the trial, the judicial personnel may, based on the specific circumstances of the case, ask questions regarding the key facts of conviction and sentencing to ascertain the voluntariness and authenticity of the defendant's admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, and confirm whether the defendant committed the crime and whether he or she genuinely repents.
If the defendant admitted guilt and accepted punishment against his or her will, or subsequently retracts after admitting guilt and accepting punishment, and a change of procedure is required according to law, the case shall be retried in accordance with the ordinary procedure. If acts of illegal evidence collection such as extortion of confessions by torture are discovered, they shall be handled in accordance with the law.
40. Adoption of Sentencing Recommendations. The People's Court shall review the sentencing recommendation proposed by the People's Procuratorate according to law. If the facts are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, the charges are accurate, and the sentencing recommendation is appropriate, the People's Court shall adopt it. It shall not be adopted in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The defendant's conduct does not constitute a crime or criminal liability shall not be pursued;
(2) The defendant admitted guilt and accepted punishment against his or her will;
(3) The defendant denies the criminal facts alleged in the indictment;
(4) The charges in the indictment are inconsistent with the charges determined by the trial;
(5) Other circumstances that may affect a fair trial.
If the facts alleged in the indictment are clear, the sentencing recommendation is appropriate, but the charges in the indictment are inconsistent with the charges determined by the trial, the People's Court may hear the opinions of the People's Procuratorate, the defendant, and his or her defender regarding the charges determined by the trial, and render a judgment according to law.
If the People's Court does not adopt the sentencing recommendation of the People's Procuratorate, it shall state the reasons and basis.
41. Adjustment of Sentencing Recommendations. If, upon trial, the People's Court finds that the sentencing recommendation is clearly inappropriate, or if the defendant or defender has a justified objection to the sentencing recommendation, the People's Court shall inform the People's Procuratorate, and the People's Procuratorate may adjust the sentencing recommendation. If the People's Court finds that the adjusted sentencing recommendation is appropriate, it shall adopt it; if the People's Procuratorate does not adjust the sentencing recommendation or the adjusted recommendation remains clearly inappropriate, the People's Court shall render a judgment according to law.
For cases tried using the fast-track procedure, the People's Procuratorate shall propose the adjustment of the sentencing recommendation before trial or during the trial. If, after adjusting the sentencing recommendation, the defendant agrees to continue applying the fast-track procedure, no change of procedure is required.
42. Conditions for Applying the Fast-Track Procedure. For a case under the jurisdiction of a Primary People's Court where the likely sentence is fixed-term imprisonment of three years or less, if the facts of the case are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, the defendant admits guilt and accepts punishment, and agrees to the application of the fast-track procedure, the fast-track procedure may be applied, and the case shall be tried by a single judge. When initiating a public prosecution, the People's Procuratorate may recommend that the People's Court apply the fast-track procedure.
The fast-track procedure shall not be applied in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The defendant is blind, deaf, mute, or a person with a mental illness who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his or her behavior;
(2) The defendant is a minor;
(3) The case has a significant social impact;
(4) In a joint crime case, some defendants object to the criminal facts, charges, sentencing recommendation, or the application of the fast-track procedure;
(5) The defendant and the victim or the victim's legal representative have not reached a mediation or settlement agreement on matters such as compensation for ancillary civil actions;
(6) Other cases where the fast-track procedure is not suitable.
43. Time Limit for Trial Under the Fast-Track Procedure. When using the fast-track procedure to try a case, the People's Court shall conclude the trial within ten days from the date of acceptance; if the likely sentence of fixed-term imprisonment exceeds one year, the trial shall be concluded within fifteen days.
44. Trial Procedure for Fast-Track Cases. When using the fast-track procedure to try a case, the restrictions on service periods specified in the Criminal Procedure Law shall not apply; generally, no court investigation or court debate is conducted, but before the judgment is pronounced, the opinions of the defender and the defendant's final statement shall be heard.
When the People's Court uses the fast-track procedure to try a case, it may serve the notice of rights and obligations and the summons of the trial together with the indictment upon the defendant, and verify the defendant's basic personal information, etc. Where necessary, service may be made centrally.
When the People's Court uses the fast-track procedure to try cases, it may hold centralized hearings and try cases one by one. The People's Procuratorate may assign public prosecutors to appear in court centrally to support the public prosecution. After the public prosecutor briefly reads the indictment, the judicial personnel shall ask the defendant in court for his or her opinion on the alleged facts, evidence, sentencing recommendation, and the application of the fast-track procedure, verify the voluntariness, authenticity, and legality of the signing of the affidavit, and verify the compensation for ancillary civil actions, etc.
When using the fast-track procedure to try a case, the judgment shall be pronounced in court. For centralized hearings, judgments may be pronounced centrally in court. When pronouncing the judgment, based on the needs of the case, the judicial personnel may conduct legal education in court. The judgment document may be simplified.
45. Second Instance Procedure for Fast-Track Cases. If a defendant appeals against a first-instance judgment rendered using the fast-track procedure, the case may be tried without a hearing. After review by the second-instance People's Court, the case shall be handled according to the following circumstances respectively:
(1) If it is found that the defendant appealed on the grounds of unclear facts or insufficient evidence, the court shall render a ruling to set aside the original judgment and remand the case to the original trial court for a new trial using the ordinary procedure, and no longer grant lenient punishment as a case involving admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment;
(2) If it is found that the defendant appealed on the grounds of an inappropriate sentence, if the original sentence was appropriate, the court shall render a ruling to dismiss the appeal and affirm the original judgment; if the original sentence was inappropriate, the court shall, after trial, modify the judgment according to law.
46. Application of the Summary Procedure. For a case under the jurisdiction of a Primary People's Court where the defendant admits guilt and accepts punishment, if the facts are clear, the evidence is sufficient, and the defendant has no objection to the application of the summary procedure, the summary procedure may be applied for trial.
When using the summary procedure to try a case involving admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, the public prosecutor may briefly read the indictment, and the judicial personnel shall ask the defendant in court for his or her opinion on the criminal facts alleged, evidence, sentencing recommendation, and the application of the summary procedure, and verify the voluntariness, authenticity, and legality of the signing of the affidavit. Court investigation may be simplified, but disputed facts and evidence shall be investigated and cross-examined. Court debate may be limited to disputed issues. The judgment document may be simplified.
47. Application of the Ordinary Procedure. When using the ordinary procedure to handle a case involving admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, the court investigation and debate procedures may be appropriately simplified. After the public prosecutor reads the indictment, the collegial panel shall ask the defendant in court for his or her opinion on the criminal facts alleged, evidence, and sentencing recommendation, and verify the voluntariness, authenticity, and legality of the signing of the affidavit. The questioning and interrogation of the defendant by the public prosecutor, defender, and judicial personnel may be simplified. For evidence to which both the prosecution and defense do not object, only the name and probative content of the evidence may be explained; for evidence to which the prosecution and defense object, or which the court considers necessary to investigate and verify, the evidence shall be presented and cross-examined. Court debate shall mainly focus on disputed issues, and the judgment document may be appropriately simplified.
48. Change of Procedure. If, during the trial using the fast-track procedure, the People's Court discovers that the defendant's conduct does not constitute a crime or that criminal liability shall not be pursued, that the defendant admitted guilt and accepted punishment against his or her will, or that the defendant denies the criminal facts alleged, the case shall be changed to the ordinary procedure for trial. If other circumstances are discovered that make the fast-track procedure unsuitable but the conditions for applying the summary procedure are met, the case shall be changed to the summary procedure for a new trial.
If circumstances are discovered that make the summary procedure unsuitable, the case shall be changed to the ordinary procedure for trial.
If, during the trial using the fast-track procedure by the People's Court, the People's Procuratorate discovers circumstances that make the fast-track procedure unsuitable, it shall recommend that the People's Court change the case to the ordinary procedure or the summary procedure for a new trial; if circumstances are discovered that make the summary procedure unsuitable, it shall recommend that the People's Court change to the ordinary procedure for a new trial.
49. Handling Cases Where the Defendant Admits Guilt and Accepts Punishment in Court. If the defendant did not admit guilt and accept punishment during the investigation or review of prosecution stage, but admits guilt in court and is willing to accept punishment, the People's Court shall, based on the facts ascertained through trial, hear the opinions of the prosecution and defense on conviction and sentencing, and render a judgment according to law.
50. Handling Cases Where the Defendant Admits Guilt and Accepts Punishment During the Second Instance Procedure. If the defendant did not admit guilt and accept punishment during the first instance procedure but does so during the second instance procedure, the trial procedure shall be conducted in accordance with the second instance procedure specified in the Criminal Procedure Law. The second-instance People's Court shall decide whether to grant leniency based on the value and role of the admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment, and shall render a judgment according to law. The determination of the range of leniency shall differ from that for admitting guilt and accepting punishment during the first instance procedure.
